<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Samoa</h1><p class="st-meta">1962 CE – present · capital Apia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Polynesian island country, independent from New Zealand since 1962, a parliamentary democracy whose politics rests on the chiefly matai system.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-western-samoa">Territory of Western Samoa</a> was administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> from 1914, as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a> and later a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory. After repeated efforts by the independence movement, the Western Samoa Act of 24 November 1961 ended the trusteeship, and Western Samoa became independent on 1 January 1962.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The 1960 constitution came into force with independence and builds on the British parliamentary pattern, modified for Samoan custom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiame-mataafa-faumuina-mulinuu-ii">Fiamē Mataʻafa Faumuina Mulinuʻu II</a> became the first prime minister, while two paramount chiefs, Tupua Tamasese Meaʻole and Malietoa Tanumafili II, were joint heads of state for life. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">Universal suffrage</a> was adopted in 1990, but only chiefs (matai) may stand for the Samoan seats in the Fono. The country joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1976 and dropped Western from its name on 4 July 1997. The economy has depended on agriculture and fishing, with aid, family remittances and tourism now important.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Tuimalealiʻifano Vaʻaletoʻa Sualauvi II has been head of state since 2017. In 2017 Parliament amended Article 1 of the constitution to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> the state religion. In May 2021 Fiamē Naomi Mataʻafa became the first female prime minister, ending the 22-year premiership of Tuilaʻepa Saʻilele Malielegaoi, and in 2025 Laʻauli Leuatea Schmidt replaced her after a snap election.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/samoa.jpg" alt="Samoa" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Original: Malietoa Tanumafili II and Tupua Tamasese Meaʻole Vector: See the file history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Samoa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1962 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Apia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fiamē Mataʻafa Faumuina Mulinuʻu II, Tuilaʻepa Saʻilele Malielegaoi, Fiamē Naomi Mataʻafa, Laʻauli Leuatea Schmidt</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christian (Congregational Church of Samoa, Roman Catholic, Latter-day Saints, Methodist, Assembly of God)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Samoan, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/samoa">Samoa</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Independent State of Samoa, Western Samoa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1994&amp;focus=samoa" aria-label="Samoa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="-27 2693 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Samoa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M293 2949L295 2949"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M295 2949h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M293 2949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M293 2949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Apia. <a href="/atlas?year=1994&amp;focus=samoa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
